quebec-based medicago's kovafens is a  point of canadian pride it's the first  homegrown coven 19 vaccine to be  approved by health canada and the first  plant-derived vaccine to hit the market  ever but now it's facing a disappointing  rejection by the who which warned this  would happen it's well known that double  joe and u.n has a very strict policy  regarding the  engagement with tobacco and arms  industry medicago is partially owned by  the world's largest tobacco company  phillip morris international maricago  says it's been trying to distance itself  since july of 2020. takashi negao  mericago's president and ceo said in a  statement the company is aware the  vaccine status is not accepted and that  the decision was linked to its minority  shareholder not as a result of kovafen's  safety or efficacy menicago is still  waiting on details and rationale before  taking next steps  canada has a deal for 76 million doses  of cova fence they plan to donate many  of them to the global vaccine sharing  program known as kovacs the rejection by  the who will make donations difficult  bioethicist carrie bowman says the need  to make vaccines available to those who  haven't yet received them should  outweigh any ethics issues the who has  with medical's ties to the tobacco giant  one of the challenges particularly for  low-income countries it's not just  supply anymore it's infrastructure and  it's vaccine hesitancy we need every  single tool we can get and this is a  very good vaccine  and it's got a lot of benefits  particularly for some not all but people  that are vaccine hesitant canada has  promised 200 million doses to kovacs so  far just 37 million have been donated  the federal government says it's working  with medical to come up with a solution  in canada cove offends is approved for  ages 18 to 64. jamie morocco global news  toronto
 

